---
import Article from "../../../layouts/Article.astro";
import KeyTakeaway from "../../../components/KeyTakeaway.astro";
import InfoBox from "../../../components/InfoBox.astro";
import ComparisonTable from "../../../components/ComparisonTable.astro";
---

<Article
  title="Duel of Meloch vs Scythe: How the Duel Actually Differs"
  description="Same universe, same components, a completely different length of evening."
  game="duel-of-meloch"
  gameTitle="Duel of Meloch"
  category="Guide"
  publishDate="2026-08-09"
>
  <p>
    If you already own Scythe, the useful question isn't "what is Duel of Meloch"
    — it's "how different is it, and does it replace anything?" The short answer
    is that it shares a universe and a component language with Scythe but sets out
    to do something Scythe has never done well: be quick.
  </p>

  <KeyTakeaway answer="Scythe is a 90–120 minute multiplayer strategy game. Duel of Meloch is a 45-minute head-to-head race on a tiny map. It doesn't replace Scythe — it covers the evenings Scythe can't." />

  <h2>Side by Side</h2>

  <ComparisonTable title="Duel of Meloch vs Scythe">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th></th>
          <th>Duel of Meloch</th>
          <th>Scythe</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Players</strong></td>
          <td>2 only</td>
          <td>1–5 (up to 7 with expansions)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Play time</strong></td>
          <td>~45 minutes</td>
          <td>90–120 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Map</strong></td>
          <td>Compact, double-sided, 10 locations + 2 lakes</td>
          <td>Large territory map</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Win condition</strong></td>
          <td>First to 4 stars on a shared Triumph Track</td>
          <td>6 stars ends the game, then coin scoring</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Asymmetry</strong></td>
          <td>Between two <em>games</em> — Scythe vs Expeditions</td>
          <td>Between factions and player mats</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Catch-up</strong></td>
          <td>Built in — your stars feed your opponent</td>
          <td>Minimal; leaders tend to stay leaders</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Standalone</strong></td>
          <td>Yes</td>
          <td>Yes</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </ComparisonTable>

  <h2>The Three Differences That Actually Matter</h2>

  <div class="my-6 space-y-3">
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-clay/10 text-clay text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">1</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Four stars, not six</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Cutting the star requirement is what compresses the game. In Scythe, six stars means a long arc where you build an engine and then convert it. At four, you're converting almost immediately — there's no comfortable build-up phase to hide in.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-clay/10 text-clay text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">2</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Ten locations means contact is immediate</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Scythe's map gives you room to develop before anyone can reach you. On a ten-location board there is no such room. Positioning matters from the first turn and you're never far from your opponent.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-clay/10 text-clay text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">3</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Leading has a cost</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Every star you place hands your opponent a Meloch card and a choice of reward. Scythe rewards getting ahead and staying ahead; Meloch taxes it. That single rule changes how you pace a game more than anything else in the box.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <InfoBox type="rule" title="Everything crosses over">
    Your existing Scythe factions, player mats, encounter cards and factory cards
    all work inside Duel of Meloch. Constantinople and the Adventurous mat work in
    regular Scythe. Nothing in either box is walled off from the other.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Does It Replace Scythe at Two Players?</h2>

  <p>
    For most people, yes — and that's a compliment to Meloch rather than a
    criticism of Scythe.
  </p>

  <p>
    Scythe at two players works, but it's a large map with a lot of empty space
    and a long game arc designed to absorb more players. Two-player Scythe tends
    to be a parallel build with occasional friction. Duel of Meloch is
    purpose-built for the head-to-head and it shows: tighter map, faster clock,
    and a win condition that keeps both players in it.
  </p>

  <p>
    What it doesn't replace is Scythe's <em>scale</em> — the sense of a wide board
    filling up, multiple factions colliding, the long game of positioning toward
    six stars. If that's what you love about Scythe, this is a different pleasure,
    not a substitute.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="tip" title="The honest framing">
    Think of it as the weeknight version. Scythe is the game you plan an evening
    around; Duel of Meloch is the one you can actually get to the table on a
    Tuesday. Most Scythe owners will find they play the small box more often and
    still keep the big one.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Which Should a Newcomer Buy?</h2>

  <p>
    If you've never played either and you mostly game as a pair, start with Duel
    of Meloch — it's shorter, cheaper to commit to, and standalone. Be aware
    you're learning two asymmetric systems at once, which is a real ramp.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you game in groups of three or four, buy <a href="/games/scythe/">Scythe</a>.
    Meloch is strictly two-player and won't serve your table.
  </p>

  <p>
    The full buying case is in
    <a href="/games/duel-of-meloch/is-duel-of-meloch-worth-it/">is Duel of Meloch worth it</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
